<h3>CHAPTER 1: Physics of Ionizing Radiation & Quantities/Units</h3>

<p>Ionizing radiation possesses sufficient energy to detach electrons from atoms or molecules, creating charged ions. Understanding physical quantities and measurement units is fundamental to quantifying dose and assessing risk.</p>

<h4>1.1 Fundamental Radiation Quantities and Units</h4>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Quantity</th>
      <th>SI Unit</th>
      <th>Traditional Unit</th>
      <th>Physical Definition & Clinical Context</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Exposure</strong></td>
      <td>Coulomb per kilogram (C/kg)</td>
      <td>Roentgen (R)</td>
      <td>Measures ionization density produced by X-rays/gamma rays in a volume of air.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Absorbed Dose ($D$)</strong></td>
      <td>Gray (Gy) [1 Gy = 1 J/kg]</td>
      <td>Rad (1 Gy = 100 rad)</td>
      <td>Energy deposited per unit mass of matter (tissue). $1 \text{ mGy} = 0.001 \text{ Gy}$.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Equivalent Dose ($H_T$)</strong></td>
      <td>Sievert (Sv)</td>
      <td>Rem (1 Sv = 100 rem)</td>
      <td>Adjusts absorbed dose for the biological effectiveness of specific radiation types ($H_T = D \times w_R$). For diagnostic X-rays, Radiation Weighting Factor ($w_R$) = 1.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Effective Dose ($E$)</strong></td>
      <td>Sievert (Sv)</td>
      <td>Rem</td>
      <td>Adjusts equivalent dose for variations in biological radiosensitivity across organ systems ($E = \sum H_T \times w_T$). Reflects total body stochastic risk.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

