<h3>CHAPTER 2: Radiobiology: Cellular Damage Mechanisms & Biological Risks</h3>

<p>Biological damage results from direct or indirect ionization of cellular targets, primarily intracellular DNA molecules.</p>

<h4>2.1 DNA Damage Mechanisms</h4>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Direct Effect:</strong> Radiation photon directly interacts with and ruptures essential cellular macromolecules (DNA strands). Dominant mechanism for high Linear Energy Transfer (LET) particulate radiation.</li>
  <li><strong>Indirect Effect:</strong> Radiation interacts with intracellular water molecules ($H_2O$), inducing radiolytic cleavage to form free radicals ($\text{OH}^-$, $H^+$). These highly reactive free radicals diffuse to attack DNA strands. Dominant mechanism (~$70\%$) for low-LET radiation like diagnostic X-rays.</li>
</ul>

<h4>2.2 Deterministic vs. Stochastic Risk Comparison</h4>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Characteristic</th>
      <th>Deterministic Effects (Tissue Reactions)</th>
      <th>Stochastic Effects (Probabilistic)</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Dose Threshold</strong></td>
      <td>Yes (Clear threshold dose must be exceeded).</td>
      <td>No threshold (Linear No-Threshold - LNT model).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Severity vs. Dose</strong></td>
      <td>Severity increases directly with higher dose.</td>
      <td>Severity is independent of dose (all-or-nothing response).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Probability vs. Dose</strong></td>
      <td>Zero below threshold; 100% above threshold.</td>
      <td>Probability increases linearly with higher dose.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Clinical Examples</strong></td>
      <td>Skin erythema (&gt;2 Gy), Cataracts (&gt;0.5 Gy), Epilation (&gt;3 Gy).</td>
      <td>Radiation-induced cancer (leukemia, solid tumors), hereditary mutations.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="illustration-box">
  <strong>[Illustration 2.1 Placement & Prompt]</strong><br/>
  <em>Description: Comparative graph showing Linear No-Threshold (LNT) model for stochastic effects vs Threshold model for deterministic tissue reactions.</em><br/>
