<h3>CHAPTER 3: International Dose Limits & Occupational Safety</h3>

<p>The International Commission on Radiological Protection (ICRP) establishes regulatory dose limits to prevent deterministic effects completely and limit stochastic risk to acceptable societal levels.</p>

<h4>3.1 Regulatory Annual Occupational & Public Dose Limits (ICRP 103)</h4>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Target Group / Anatomical Region</th>
      <th>Occupational Limit (Radiation Workers)</th>
      <th>Public / Non-Radiation Personnel Limit</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Effective Dose (Whole Body)</strong></td>
      <td>20 mSv/year (averaged over 5 years; max 50 mSv in any single year)</td>
      <td>1 mSv/year</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Lens of the Eye</strong></td>
      <td>20 mSv/year (averaged over 5 years; max 50 mSv in 1 year)</td>
      <td>15 mSv/year</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Skin (1 cm² area)</strong></td>
      <td>500 mSv/year</td>
      <td>50 mSv/year</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Hands, Feet & Extremities</strong></td>
      <td>500 mSv/year</td>
      <td>50 mSv/year</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Declared Pregnant Worker (Fetal Limit)</strong></td>
      <td>1 mSv equivalent dose to abdomen for remainder of pregnancy</td>
      <td>N/A</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<br/>

<h3>CHAPTER 4: Practical Shielding, Area Monitoring & Personal Dosimetry</h3>

<h4>4.1 Personal Dosimetry Technology</h4>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Thermoluminescent Dosimeter (TLD):</strong> Contains Lithium Fluoride (LiF) crystals. Absorbed energy traps electrons; heating releases light proportional to dose. Reusable but destroys readout data during processing.</li>
  <li><strong>Optically Stimulated Luminescence (OSL):</strong> Contains Aluminum Oxide ($Al_2O_3$). Stimulated with green laser light to emit blue light proportional to dose. Highly sensitive (down to $0.01\text{ mSv}$) and allows re-analysis.</li>
  <li><strong>Dosimeter Placement:</strong> Worn at chest/collar level. When wearing a lead apron, the primary dosimeter must be worn <em>under</em> the apron (whole-body dose); an optional second dosimeter is worn <em>outside</em> at collar level (eye/head dose).</li>
</ul>

<h4>4.2 Shielding & Barrier Design</h4>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Barrier Type</th>
      <th>Target Radiation Source</th>
      <th>Standard Structural Requirement</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Primary Radiation Barrier</strong></td>
      <td>Direct useful X-ray beam emerging from gantry/collimator.</td>
      <td>1.5 to 2.0 mm Lead (Pb) equivalent embedded in walls up to a height of 2.1 meters.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Secondary Radiation Barrier</strong></td>
      <td>Leakage radiation from tube housing and scatter radiation from patient.</td>
      <td>0.8 to 1.0 mm Lead (Pb) equivalent (e.g., control booth walls, viewing windows).</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="illustration-box">
  <strong>[Illustration 4.1 Placement & Prompt]</strong><br/>
