<h3>CHAPTER 5: Pediatric & Pregnancy Radiation Safety Protocols</h3>

<h4>5.1 Pediatric Radiation Protection ("Image Gently" Campaign)</h4>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Increased Radiosensitivity:</strong> Children are 2 to 10 times more sensitive to radiation-induced stochastic risks than adults due to higher cellular proliferation rates and longer remaining lifespan.</li>
  <li><strong>Technical Protocol Adaptations:</strong>
    <ul>
      <li>Remove anti-scatter grid for infants/small children (body thickness &lt;10–12 cm) to reduce dose by 50%+.</li>
      <li>Utilize anatomical pediatric pre-set protocols (lower kVp and mAs auto-adjusted).</li>
      <li>Tighten collimation strictly to the region of interest.</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
</ul>

<h4>5.2 Pregnant Patient Radiation Guidelines ("Ten-Day Rule")</h4>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Gestation Period</th>
      <th>Radiation Sensitivity & Biological Risk</th>
      <th>Action Threshold / Guidelines</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Pre-Implantation (0–2 weeks)</strong></td>
      <td>"All-or-Nothing" phenomenon. Embryonic death or intact survival with no anomalies.</td>
      <td>Dose threshold ~50–100 mGy. Diagnostic exams rarely reach this level.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Organogenesis (2–8 weeks)</strong></td>
      <td>High risk of structural congenital malformations, microcephaly, growth retardation.</td>
      <td>Threshold ~100 mGy. Shield abdomen if procedure is non-pelvic; consider MRI/US alternatives.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Fetal Period (8–15 weeks)</strong></td>
      <td>High sensitivity of central nervous system (severe intellectual disability risk).</td>
      <td>Threshold ~100–300 mGy. IQ drop ~25 points per Gy above threshold.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Late Fetal Period (&gt;25 weeks)</strong></td>
      <td>Mainly stochastic risk of childhood cancer (leukemia).</td>
      <td>Minimize abdominal exposures; perform only for critical, life-threatening maternal indications.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

